2 ADDR
3 CITY Москва (Moscow)
3 CTRY Российская империя (Russian empire)
1 OCCU Deputy Governor of Vilna
1 TITL Prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Aleksy Iwan Trubecki h. Pogoń Litewska''', also known as '''Alexei Iwanowitch Troubetzkoy''', was born on May 19/7, 1806, in a noble family of Prince Iwan Nicolaewitch Troubetzkoy and Natalia Sergeiewna Meshcher
2 CONC skaya. He was the second son of the couple, and his family was one of the oldest and most influential noble families in Lithuania, Poland, and Russia. He grew up in the Troubetzkoy family estate of Achtyrka near Khotkovo near Serg
2 CONC iyev Posad near Moscow in Russia, where he received his early education. From a young age, Aleksy showed an interest in politics and soon became involved in the Polish-Lithuanian independence movement. As a young man, Aleksy was a
2 CONC  bright and ambitious individual, who received a good education. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aleksy Trubecki spent his early years in the service of the Russian Empire, working as Acting State Councilor. In 1825, he joined the Russian army and was stationed in Wilno, Vilna Governorate (now Vilnius, Lithuania). In 1830, wh
2 CONC en the Polish November Uprising broke out, Aleksy was the Vice-Governor of Wilno. During the Polish-Lithuanian November Uprising (1830 – 1831), he secretly supported the insurgents and worked as a spy for the Rząd Narodowy, govern
2 CONC ment formed by the Polish insurgents. He was a close friend of Michał Gedeon Radziwiłł, a prominent Polish-Lithuanian nobleman and military commander who led the insurgent forces, and was one of the leaders of the uprising. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the defeat of the November Uprising, Aleksy Trubecki continued to work for the Polish-Lithuanian resistance movement as a secret agent. For the next 24 years, he moved back and forth between Lithuania, Poland, and Russia, ga
2 CONC thering intelligence and supporting the underground resistance. He played an important role in the underground activities of the Polish and Lithuanian rebels, providing them with intelligence and logistical support. Despite being 
2 CONC under constant surveillance, he was never caught by the authorities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT However, despite his successful espionage career, Aleksy's activities were eventually uncovered by the Russian authorities. His luck ran out in 1855 when he was finally arrested by the Ohranka, the Russian secret police. Aleksy wa
2 CONC s imprisoned and subjected to brutal interrogation, but he refused to reveal any information about his fellow conspirators. He refused to betray his colleagues and was sentenced to death. On the Catholic Christmas Eve, December 24
2 CONC , 1855 (December 12 by the Russian old style calendar), he was executed by firing squad along with several other Polish or Lithuanian rebels. His execution was a significant loss to the Polish-Lithuanian resistance movement, and h
2 CONC e became a martyr to the Polish-Lithuanian cause. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aleksy was survived by his wife, Nadezhda Borisovna Svyatopolk-Chetvertinskaya, whom he married in 1843, and his two children, Natalia (Princess Shakhovskaya), and Alexey. His daughter Natalia married Prince Nikolai Ivanovich Shak
2 CONC hovskoy, who was a prominent Russian nobleman and statesman. His son Alexey Trubetskoy married Natalia Dmitrievna Vsevolzhskaya and had offspring from the marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aleksy was buried in the Church of St. Nicholas the Wonderworker in Gnezdniki, located at: Bolshoy Gnezdnikovsky Lane 4, Moscow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his tragic end, Aleksy Iwan Trubecki remains a symbol of courage and patriotism for the Lithuanian and Polish people. His bravery and commitment to the cause of Polish and Lithuanian independence inspired many to continue 
2 CONC the fight against Russian oppression. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, Aleksy Iwan Trubecki is remembered as a hero of the Polish-Lithuanian resistance movement, who risked his life to support the cause of Lithuanian and Polish independence. His legacy continues to inspire people who fight for
2 CONC  freedom and human rights around the World. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------- 
